.DS_Store
# Downloaded and quantized weights. Anchored to the repository root so nested
# documentation directories are not ignored.
/models/
/quantized_models/
.idea/
.vscode/
.DS_Store

# Profiler traces. Nsight Systems reports are large binaries (a 90-second vLLM capture is
# ~138 MB), which exceeds GitHub's 100 MB per-file limit. Keep them out of git and store them
# in S3; the summary tables that answer the actual question are committed as text alongside
# the corresponding benchmark_results_*.txt.
*.nsys-rep
*.qdrep
*.sqlite
docs/results/traces/

# MLPerf LoadGen writes its own logs (summary, detail, trace) per invocation. The runner
# points these into docs/results/.../reps/ for real runs; this catches ad-hoc invocations.
loadgen_out/
mlperf_log_*.txt
mlperf_log_*.json

# Benchmark results are private to whoever ran them. `suite` and `launch` write one .txt per run
# into docs/results/, and those files carry the endpoint, cluster context, served model path and
# pod names of the environment they were measured in. They stay local: a result describes your
# infrastructure, not the harness. docs/results/README.md documents the format and is tracked.
docs/results/*.txt

# Written reports are private for the same reason and one more: they interpret the results above,
# quoting numbers measured on a particular customer's cluster. Each directory's README.md stays
# tracked and documents the format, so the conventions are public and the measurements are not.
docs/reports/*.md
docs/reports/reruns
docs/reports/aiperf-calibration
docs/reports/validation
!docs/reports/README.md

# Implementation plans are working documents, not deliverables. They record intent, file paths and
# line numbers for work not yet done, so they go stale the moment the work lands and they carry no
# value to anyone cloning the repo. Kept local for the same reason as the reports above.
docs/plans/

# Accuracy references and prompt token counts are measurements too, not configuration. A reference
# is your server's own output, and is only valid ground truth for a server you verified yourself;
# a token count belongs to the exact tokenizer release that produced it. Both are cheap to
# regenerate — `accuracy.py --write-reference` and `verify-profiles` — and each directory's
# README.md explains how. The READMEs also keep the directories present in a clone, which the
# launcher's payload packing requires.
docs/accuracy/*.json
docs/prompt_tokens/*.json

# Build metadata from `pip install -e .`
*.egg-info/
